<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <meta name="robots" content="noindex, follow">

  <!--
    CONTACT -> BOARD REDIRECT

    The email address is gone; /contact/ is now /board/. This page stays so
    old links, bookmarks, and anything already indexed still land somewhere
    useful instead of a 404.

    GitHub Pages can't issue a 301, so this does it three ways: a canonical
    tag for crawlers, a meta refresh for browsers with JS off, and a
    replace() for everyone else (replace, not assign, so Back doesn't bounce).
  -->
  <link rel="canonical" href="https://durtnurs.com/board/">
  <meta http-equiv="refresh" content="0; url=/board/">
  <title>Moved to the board - tHE dURT nURS'</title>

  <style>
    body {
      margin: 0;
      min-height: 100vh;
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;
      padding: 2rem;
      text-align: center;
      background-color: #0B0B0C;
      color: #E8E6E3;
      font-family: 'Merriweather', Georgia, serif;
      line-height: 1.6;
    }

    h1 {
      font-family: 'Oswald', 'Arial Narrow', sans-serif;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: #A05A24;
      margin: 0 0 1rem;
    }

    a {
      color: #8B7A43;
    }
  </style>

  <script>
    window.location.replace('/board/');
  </script>
</head>
<body>
  <main>
    <h1>We took the email down</h1>
    <p>
      There's a board now. You're being walked over to it.
    </p>
    <p>
      If nothing happens, <a href="/board/">go to the board yourself</a>.
    </p>
  </main>
</body>
</html>
